post_install() {
  cat <<'MSG'
nekopull ships a systemd user service that polls nekoBT continuously and
watches ~/.config/nekopull/config.toml for changes. To switch it on:

  systemctl --user enable --now nekopull.service

Start from /usr/share/doc/nekopull/config.example.toml.
MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
  post_install
  # $2 is the version being replaced; only spell out the 0.3.0 migration when
  # coming from a timer-based release.
  if [[ -n ${2:-} ]] && (( $(vercmp "$2" 0.3.0) < 0 )); then
    cat <<'MSG'

0.3.0 replaces the daily timer with this service. If you had the timer
enabled, drop it and start the daemon instead:

  systemctl --user disable --now nekopull.timer
  systemctl --user enable --now nekopull.service

That first command may report that nekopull.timer does not exist: the unit is
gone from this version, though the command still removes the leftover enable
symlink. If the warning is all you get and the symlink survives, delete it by
hand:  rm ~/.config/systemd/user/timers.target.wants/nekopull.timer

Unlike the timer, the daemon keeps the code it started with, so restart it
after an upgrade:

  systemctl --user restart nekopull.service
MSG
  fi
}
